      {"field": "plain_english", "severity": "Medium", "issue": "The plain_english explains the name wrongly: \"It's called 'linear' because the slider's position maps directly to the level.\" 'Linear' actually refers to the straight-line physical travel (vs a rotary knob). Saying position 'maps directly to the level' asserts a proportional mapping, which contradicts the entry's own definition (a logarithmic 'fader law' in dB) and directly seeds the exact misconception the common_mistakes list then warns against ('assuming the fader's taper is linear in dB'). A beginner is left with a self-contradicting mental model.", "suggestion": "Rewrite the sentence to attach 'linear' to the motion, not the level, e.g.: \"It's called a 'linear' fader because it slides in a straight line (unlike a rotary knob). Its calibrated travel is marked in dB, so equal-looking moves near the bottom change the level far more than moves near the top.\""}
